'''Nasal vowels''' (marked with tilde: ã, ẽ, ĩ, õ, ũ) occur primarily in words of indigenous origin and in some Portuguese-derived vocabulary.

===Stress===
Stress in Baliscano is generally predictable:
* '''Words ending in a vowel, -n, or -s:''' Stress falls on the penultimate syllable (''KA-sa'' "house", ''ka-SA-s'' "houses")
* '''Words ending in other consonants:''' Stress falls on the ultimate syllable (''a-MAR'' "to love")
* '''Exceptions:''' Marked with an acute accent (''ká-fe'' "coffee")

==Grammar==
{{Main|Baliscano grammar}}

Baliscano grammar exhibits typical creole features, including analytic morphology, pre-verbal TMA marking, and relatively fixed SVO word order. However, two centuries of nativization and standardization have added complexity to the basic creole structure.

===Tense-Mood-Aspect (TMA) system===
The TMA system is central to Baliscano grammar. Tense, mood, and aspect are marked by pre-verbal particles rather than inflection:

{| class="wikitable"
|-
! Particle !! Function !! Example !! Translation
|-
| ''(unmarked)'' || present || ''Yo kome.'' || "I eat." / "I am eating."
|-
| ''ya'' || past/completive || ''Yo ya kome.'' || "I ate." / "I have eaten."
|-
| ''lo'' || future || ''Yo lo kome.'' || "I will eat."
|-
| ''sta'' || progressive || ''Yo sta kome.'' || "I am eating."
|-
| ''soli'' || habitual || ''Yo soli kome.'' || "I usually eat."
|-
| ''kaba'' || completive || ''Yo kaba kome.'' || "I have just eaten."
|-
| ''taba'' || imperfect || ''Yo taba kome.'' || "I was eating."
|}

TMA particles can be combined:
* ''ya sta kome'' – "I was eating" (past progressive)
* ''lo kaba kome'' – "I will have eaten" (future perfect)

===Nouns and noun phrases===
Baliscano nouns are not marked for case. Key features include:

'''Number:''' Plurality is optionally marked with ''-s'' (vowel-final) or ''-es'' (consonant-final):
* ''amigo'' → ''amigos'' "friend(s)"
* ''mujer'' → ''mujeres'' "woman/women"

'''Definiteness:''' Marked by articles:
* ''el'' (masc. sg.), ''la'' (fem. sg.), ''los'' (masc. pl.), ''las'' (fem. pl.)

'''Gender:''' Baliscano has '''natural gender''' rather than grammatical gender. Inanimate nouns are generally treated as masculine unless derived from a feminine form.

